    // WALKER DELEGATION (this PR): every rule below was blind to a POSITION, not to a shape. The four
    // lambda-body cases (ownership use-after-consume, consume INSIDE the lambda, sendable, must-use)
    // are one defect — a `_` arm that omitted `.Lambda` — and the leak cases are the same defect on
    // statement lists: an owner bound in a nested block/arm/lambda leaked silently. The escape cases
    // close the laundering paths AROUND the (already exhaustive) predicate: out through an invoked
    // FnT param, in through a loop-body store, in through a loop's element binding, and out through a
    // match that extracts an enum payload. The two accepted cases pin the near-misses: a DISCARDED
    // `apply(() { x.addr() })` yields its value to a call site that throws it away (the lambda's
    // implicit trailing return is not a return from the enclosing function), and an owner released
    // inside the same lambda that created it is discharged.
    // explicit by-name import wins: `{ at } = std.text.str` designates str's at(_, i64) as the
    // authoritative `at`, so `s.at(i64)` type-checks even though ascii's at(_, i32) is pulled into
    // the flat program (str imports ascii for is_space). Regression pin for UFCS dispatch precedence.
    // the mirror: importing ascii's at(_, i32) BY NAME keeps its i32 overload — correct
    // single-provider dispatch is not regressed by the precedence rule.
    // A source ending mid-char-literal (`'` at EOF) must produce a clean positioned parse error,
    // not an OOB read past the NUL terminator (ASan heap-buffer-overflow via char_end/scan_quote_end).
    // A `.Variant` reference to an undefined name must reject cleanly; the undefined-name hint used
    // to walk the ident off a sentinel (-1) offset, reading before the flat buffer (ASan OOB).
    // out-param store escape: `stash` plants `x.addr()` through its `b` pointer param, so the local
    // leaves fill's frame via a caller-reachable location (not via the call's return value).
    // NOT an escape: the same store, but the destination (`lb`) is a LOCAL that dies with `x` — the
    // planted address never outlives the frame, so the out-param-store rule must not flag it.
    // Omitting a field whose type TRANSITIVELY holds a non-null Ptr (nested struct) zero-fills it to a
    // null the type forbids → reject, same as a direct Ptr field.
    // Enum analog: omitting an enum-typed field reads tag 0 = the first variant, whose Ptr payload is
    // zeroed to null → reject.
    // Companion: fully constructing the transitive-Ptr field is fine — the pointer is provably non-null.
    // Companion: a nested struct that only zero-fills scalars stays legal to omit (no pointer nulled).
    // #574: `.len()` on a string_view when `len` is NOT imported — the closure CONTAINS a flat
    // `len` (std.text.str rides in via the `contains` import), so this used to check clean and
    // emit a bare C `len(...)` bound by flat-namespace luck. Must be a positioned error now.
    // #574 on a USER-STRUCT receiver: same unimported-name miss, classified as method-not-found
    // (the receiver is a declared struct, so it reads as a missing method, not a bare name).
    // #574 positive control: the SAME call with `len` imported is legal UFCS on a builtin.
    // struct `==`/`!=`: mismatched types and non-comparable (slice) fields reject up front.
    // ── struct-reflection intrinsics: every ill-formed shape is REJECTED with a positioned kind,
    // never a genc leak, a compiler panic, or a silent no-op ──
    // v2 leak: allocated, READ via a pure getter (`o.get()`), then forgotten — never released,
    // moved, or returned. A pure read does NOT discharge the owner, so this LEAKS (v1 missed it,
    // exempting any owner that was named again — even by a read).
    // v2 leak: read via a getter in TRAILING (return) position — the returned value is the getter's
    // scalar result, NOT the owner, so the owner still leaks.
    // NOT a leak: read via a getter, THEN released — the release discharges it.
    // NOT a leak: read via a getter, THEN the owner ITSELF is returned (escapes) — discharged.
    // NOT flagged (conservative under-report): the owner is passed BY VALUE to a callee we do not
    // prove non-retaining — `o` in a non-getter-arg position is an impure use, so v2 stays silent.
    // NOT a leak: a PARAM owner (the caller owns it) read via a getter then released — never a
    // candidate (only fresh top-level productions are), and released anyway.
    // field-aware escape: a slice literal element carries a stack-local address (re-census #1 gap).
    // UFCS auto-ref: value Rng where next expects MutPtr<Rng> — rewrite to addr(r).
    // no over-accept: temp receiver seed(1).next() stays rejected (not an lvalue).
    // census: Color.Purple passed check then ICEd at cc (undeclared Color_Purple).
    // no over-rejection: a valid qualified tag still accepts.
    // same hole on a payload ctor: Color.Purple(1) must reject before emit.
    // generic enum: Result.Bogus must reject (enum_variants covers DGenEnum).
    // census: undefined module member in a UFCS chain (os.env) — positioned reject at check, not cc ICE.
    // MutPtr param mismatch on a non-lvalue literal — gate at check, not cc ICE.
    // POINTER-FLOOR mode: `Ptr<u8>` is read-only, not a wildcard that can become `MutPtr<u8>`.
    // POINTER-FLOOR pointee: `MutPtr<u8>` is not the raw floor and cannot become `MutPtr<i32>`.
    // RECURSIVE MODE INVARIANCE: generic wrappers, nested pointer pointees, and slice elements cannot
    // launder Ptr→MutPtr or MutPtr→Ptr.
    // The deliberately permissive floor remains `RawPtr<u8>`: null_ptr() and raw allocator results
    // are indistinguishable at this boundary and still fit typed pointers.
    // BRICK 1 discriminating PAIR (docs/effects-as-types-design.md §4): identical program modulo the
    // pointer KIND at one nested leaf. A deeply-frozen `Outer→Inner→scalars` payload (mode `frozen`,
    // reaches no MutPtr) is ACCEPTED — the runtime twin is send_frozen_payload_accepted.zen.
    // …the SAME shape with Inner's leaf turned `MutPtr<i32>` reaches a MutPtr (mode `read`), so the
    // send is REJECTED — proving `mode_of` (not an ad-hoc leaf) drives the sendability verdict.
    // RECEIVER-TYPED UFCS DISPATCH (#68 slice 4): `find` is BOTH a free fn (str-receiver) and an impl
    // method (Coll-receiver). A `[i32]` receiver fits NEITHER, so `xs.find(3)` must NOT silently bind to
    // the free str.find and report `expected str, got [i32]` — it is method-not-found for `[i32]`.
    // The dual accept: with the SAME name-collision in scope, a `Coll` receiver dispatches to the impl
    // method by receiver type — receiver-typed preference picks the fitting method, not the free str.find.
    // census #58/#41: `.field` on a generic-enum instance (Result) has no C member — was check-ok
    // then a codegen ICE ("has no member 'len'"); now a positioned struct-field reject.
    // #58/#41: `.field` on a CONCRETE enum is likewise rejected (enums reach variants via .match).
    // no over-rejection: `.match` on a Result (which lowers to `.tag`/`.u` accesses) still accepts.
    // no over-rejection: `.or_return()` unwrap (its Err-guard lowering also builds `.tag`/`.u`) accepts.
    // C1 (task #35) REJECT: a GENERIC forwarder launders a MutPtr past the send-mut check — its
    // payload EXPRESSION is the bare tparam `T` (reaches no MutPtr), but the `.Poke` message it
    // builds has a DECLARED MutPtr field, so the receiver still gets a mutable alias. Caught at the
    // forwarder's definition — the handle `h: CellRef<Msg>` pins the message type M=Msg, so the
    // constructed variant's declared payload is resolved even though the payload value is untyped.
    // C1 REJECT: the SAME launder through a MIXED enum, sending the MutPtr-carrying variant.
    // C1 ACCEPT (no over-rejection): a generic forwarder sending a VALUE-carrying variant — the
    // declared payload is an i32, reaches no MutPtr.
    // C1 ACCEPT: a generic forwarder over a MIXED enum sending the sibling VALUE variant (Inc) —
    // the MutPtr-carrying sibling (Poke) must NOT taint the safe variant.
    // C2 (task #35) REJECT: a str flowing into a CONCRETE `i32` slot of a GENERIC callee — the
    // identical non-generic call already errored; the generic one used to compile (silent
    // miscompile: a str pointer through an i32 slot).
    // C2 REJECT: a literal that overflows a CONCRETE `i8` slot of a generic callee (the
    // literal-range check was dead for generic callees too).
    // bad-binding REJECT: a single-`=` top-level scalar binding is neither a module alias (ident
    // RHS) nor a function (`(` RHS). It used to fall to fill_func and SILENTLY swallow the next
    // decl (`main` here), and `check` said "ok"; now it is a clean error[bad-binding].
    // bad-binding REJECT + no-swallow PROOF: the FIRST error is bad-binding (at `x`), NOT
    // `undefined-name` for `wanted` — i.e. `wanted*` and its `*` survive the malformed line.
    // bad-binding REJECT (ident-HEADED value RHS): `K = Ctor(1, 2)` / `K = compute()` — an ident
    //   followed by `(` is a VALUE call, not a module path, so decl_is_module_alias no longer claims
    //   it. It used to plant a void `dglobal_alias` that ICEd codegen (`Ctor`) or was silently
    //   accepted as a second spelling of `:=` (`compute`); now both are a clean error[bad-binding].
    // ACCEPT: an ident-RHS module alias (`m = std.math`) — the working (module-PATH) form — is untouched.
    // ACCEPT: a `:=` value global (the correct spelling of `k = 5`) still checks.
    // ACCEPT: a normal `(`-RHS function decl (the other single-`=` form) still checks.
    // #34 enum-variant-swallow (sibling of the bad-binding swallow, through fill_enum):
    // ACCEPT: a leading `|` before the first variant (`Status*: | Ok | Err`) is idiomatic layout
    //   — it parses as {Ok, Err} and `main` SURVIVES (before: `check` said "ok" with main swallowed).
    // REJECT (error[parse], no swallow): a double bar `| |` = an empty variant; a non-ident variant
    //   name (`5`); a mid-list empty `Ok | | Err`. fill_variant now requires an identifier name, so a
    //   stray `|`/literal in name position is a clean caretted parse error, not a silent decl-swallow.
    // #392 rejects: a value-CONSUMED match/cond arm that yields no value and does not diverge
    // is error[arm-value] — it used to reach cc as `char* _out = (done = true)` (broken C) or,
    // for the bool-subject Cond, compile SILENTLY WRONG (C `c ? "x" : bool` under -w).
    // let-RHS, enum subject, arm tail is an assignment statement.
    // let-RHS, arm is a bare call to a fn DECLARED void.
    // bool subject (Cond, C-ternary-lowered): the SILENT-WRONG twin.
    // argument position: the match flows into a CONCRETE str param slot.
    // Ok-payload mismatch in LET position (the Ok value is consumed locally, not returned):
    // ACCEPTED — only the error type must match; the lowering rebuilds the Err return as the
    // enclosing fn's Result. The headline multi-type-pipeline shape.
    // The DEGENERATE return-direct shape with a mismatched Ok payload: `return f().or_return()`
    // unwraps bool then returns it bare from a Result<i32,E> fn — rejected by the post-lowering
    // return-fit scan (not or-return: the propagation itself is fine, the leftover value isn't).
    // M1 slice 1 (rt-scoped-runtime, docs/rt-scoped-runtime.md): spawner scratch must not cross an
    // actor boundary. Rejects (error[scratch-escape]): a send whose enum payload carries a local
    // `a.acquire(…)` pointer; a send of a slice-literal-backed struct (stack storage); spawn state
    // embedding a local acquire pointer (the judges' `Counter(seen: <spawner-local>)` repro), bound
    // OR embedded directly; a send embedding an arena bound via the Result-unified
    // `make_in(…).expect(…)` (the checker unwraps `.expect` over a scratch source); AND a scratch
    // `a.acquire(…)` pointer routed out through S2's `ref_send_msg` reply (S4-lite: the reply send is
    // now a recognized scratch surface, so a pointer into the replier's OWN per-behavior scratch — reset
    // when its behavior ends — cannot escape to the receiver).
    // Accepts (no over-rejection): plain-value sends, plain-value `ref_send_msg` replies, Arc-wrapped
    // sends (`new_in(…).expect(…)` — call results classify clean), Arc-backed spawn state, and the
    // plain-value `Room(n: 7)` spawn state.
    // SENDABILITY rule 2 (Rc): an `Rc<T>` payload is rejected — its refcount is a plain i64, so two
    // actors on two threads race it — while the `Arc<T>` twin beside it is accepted. Until these two
    // cases existed, stubbing check_module_send_rc_kind to `return 0` left the ENTIRE harness green:
    // every other error[sendable] case carries a MutPtr payload, which the separate send-mut pass owns.
    // M2 (rt-scoped-runtime §4): sendability is a TYPE capability. Rejects (error[sendable]): a send
    // whose payload carries a `MutPtr<T>` (Pony `ref` — mutable + aliasable → cross-actor race), both
    // DIRECTLY and DEEP (a readonly `Ptr<Outer>` whose Outer struct reachably holds a `MutPtr` field —
    // `val` is deep). Accepts: a readonly `Ptr<T>` (`val`) payload, and a `Ptr<Outer>` whose reachable
    // struct fields are all readonly `Ptr`/value (deeply immutable). (value = pointer-free, and iso =
    // `Own<T>` move-on-send, are covered by the plain-value cases above + the ownership cases.)
    // M2 SOUNDNESS THROUGH A CALL/MEMBER + THE PARALLEL SEND PATH (close the exit-123 race): the payload
    // is classified by the TYPE it reaches (infer_expr), so a MutPtr threaded through a METHOD RETURN
    // (`h.send(.Poke(b.ptr()))`) or an IDENTITY FN (`h.send(.Poke(launder(d.addr())))`) is rejected, not
    // just a bare `.Var` — closing the `_ => false` leak. And the message of the 3-ARG parallel send
    // (`p.send(h, .Poke(mp))` — std.concurrent.pool_actor.send, message = LAST arg, not `args[1]`) is now
    // inspected, so the guarantee fires where races actually happen. Precision preserved (no over-reject):
    // a method RETURNING a readonly `Ptr<T>` (`val`) stays accepted, and a plain-value 3-arg send is clean.
    // ISO/MOVE LAUNDER: an `Own<T>` sent through a single-Own-arg identity fn (`send(.Give(launder(o)))`)
    // still move-kills the sender's binding, so a later `o.release_in(a)` is error[ownership] use-after-move
    // (else sender + actor both free `o`). A FRESH owner from a call with no Own arg (`send(.Give(mkblob(a)))`)
    // kills nothing — correctly still legal.
    // or_return in a GLOBAL initializer: there is NO enclosing function to return the Err to —
    // the lowered early-return silently aborted zen__init_globals, leaving every later global
    // zero (H stayed 0, exit 0 instead of 7). Positioned or-return error at check time.
    // Ok-payload mismatch, CONDITIONAL position (&& rhs): the lowering returns the receiver
    // VERBATIM, so a Result<bool,E> receiver in a Result<i32,E> fn emitted `return Result_bool_E`
    // inside a value block the return-fit scan never reaches → cc error as an internal compiler
    // bug. The or-return check now requires the Ok payloads to agree too.
    // Ok-payload mismatch in a @while condition — the other conditional position that dodged
    // the return-fit scan and died at cc.
    // SHADOWING is not a skip: a call through a KNOWN non-FnT param that shadows an INTRINSIC
    // (`store_i64: i64`) is not-callable — genc dispatches calls by NAME, so accepting this
    // emitted the intrinsic expansion with the value arg degraded to literal 0 (a silent 0-write).
    // same hole, cast intrinsic: `to_i64: i64` param called with 3 args ran as the CAST of the
    // first arg (extras dropped). Known non-FnT shadow → not-callable, not a silent accept.
    // SHADOWING is not a skip: calling through an i64 param that shadows a top-level fn is
    // not-callable at CHECK time (used to pass check, then die at cc: "'f' is not a function").
    // SHADOWING is not a skip, IMPORTED flavor: an i64 param shadowing an imported fn (`at`)
    // is equally not-callable — same cc-error leak class as the top-level-fn shadow.
    // RECURSIVE HOF: a self-recursive fn with an FnT param is inline-only — its residual
    // self-call after the unroll bound references a C symbol that is never emitted (link error
    // reported as an internal compiler bug). Positioned reject at check time instead.
    // recursive-hof leniency: an FnT PARAM named after the fn itself SHADOWS it — the body's
    // `apply(n)` is an indirect call through the param, not recursion. Must stay accepted.
    // SHADOWING does not blunt the checks: a wrong-arity call THROUGH the shadowed FnT param
    // (`f: (i64) i64` called with two args) is still an arity error — via the indirect-call path,
    // not top-level `f`'s signature.
    // SHADOWING does not blunt M5 R5: a wrong-arity LAMBDA passed to a HOF whose FnT param is
    // named after a top-level fn is still rejected against the HOF's declared FnT.
    // UAF alias-class join: `q := p` names the SAME allocation, so freeing p then freeing (or
    // using) q is a double-free/use-after-free — the name-only kill-set used to miss it.
    // alias UAF read: load through the alias after the original was freed.
    // alias classes are TRANSITIVE: p -> q -> r, freeing p kills r too.
    // FREE-IN-LOOP: freeing a local declared OUTSIDE the loop body frees the same pointer
    // again on the next iteration — a double-free the single lexical walk used to miss.
    // a CONDITIONAL free of an outer local in a loop MAY double-free — flagged (may-analysis,
    // the same rule that makes a branch free conditionally dead after the branch).
    // load_i64/store_i64/atomic_* deref their pointer arg exactly like load/store: a nullable
    // `RawPtr<T>` (T != u8) must be null-checked first (this used to slip through).
    // no over-rejection: REVIVAL stays per-name — rebinding the alias detaches it, so using the
    // fresh q after p's free is fine.
    // no over-rejection: freeing a loop-LOCAL is fresh each iteration.
    // no over-rejection: an alias where NEITHER name is freed until the end is fine.
    // no over-rejection: the `p = a.resize(p, n)` grow idiom rebinds in the same statement, so
    // each iteration nets to alive.
    // no over-rejection: free-then-reacquire inside the loop body revives before the iteration ends.
    // no over-rejection: the error-path idiom (free + `return` in one arm, free after) is still
    // legal — the freeing arm does not fall through.
    // MOVE-OUT OF A FIELD (audit hole): `c := b.o` (b.o an Own) moves the owner OUT of the struct —
    // the base keeps a bit-copy naming the SAME allocation, so `c.release_in(a)` then
    // `b.o.release_in(a)` was an undetected double-free. The member move kills the whole BASE
    // binding (name-keyed conservative), in every position where a bare-Var source moves today:
    // `:=`/`=` copy, a consuming Own param, an Own field/element init, a send payload, and the
    // release/drop receiver itself (`b.o.release_in(a)` kills `b`).
    // conservative kill pinned: using ANOTHER field of the base after the move-out rejects.
    // accept: move out of the field then use only the MOVED owner (`c := b.o` … `c.release_in(a)`).
    // reject: release through the field then read through the field (`b.o.release_in` → `b.o.get()`).
    // reject: the field passed to a consuming Own param (`kill(b.o, a)`) then read through the field.
    // no over-rejection: value-field reads (`b.n: i32`) and owner-field BORROW reads (`b.o.get()`)
    // are not moves — the base stays live until an actual move/consume of the field.
    // R4: a base digit out of range right after the prefix (`0o9`, `0b2`, `0b19`, `0xFFg`) used
    // to split into two tokens with the wrong value — now a positioned lexer error.
    // R5: a lone radix prefix with no digits (`0b`/`0o`/`0x`) used to be accepted as value 0.
    // Q4: an unterminated `/* */` block comment now carets its opening `/*` (was positionless).
    // a valid radix literal + a valid block comment still lex correctly (no over-rejection).
    // R2: a NESTED field write through a read-only `Ptr<T>` mutates `*b` — reject like the direct set.
    // R2 (accept): the SAME nested write through a `MutPtr<T>` is legal.
    // R3: an index-set into a slice field of a read-only `Ptr<T>` writes into `*b` — reject.
    // R3 (accept): the SAME index-set through a `MutPtr<T>` is legal.
    // accept: writing through a MutPtr field reached FROM a read-only Ptr base mutates that field's
    // target, not `*b` — the recursion stops at the first (writable) pointer indirection.
    // R6: exhaustiveness on a LOCAL-binding match subject (not a param) still rejects.
    // R7: a non-void body that falls through with no produced value is a return-fit error.
    // R1: passing a read-only `Ptr<T>` where a `MutPtr<T>` is wanted is an arg-type error.
    // R8: assert_nonnull on a non-pointer arg is an arg-type error.
    // P1 capability preservation: assert_nonnull proves only non-nullness (Ptr stays read-only),
    // and an ordinary Ptr cannot launder through writable RawPtr. RawPtr<u8> remains the explicit floor.
    // Every mutating word/atomic intrinsic observes Ptr direction, not only typed store(). Because [T]
    // is writable, slice(Ptr<T>, n) is likewise a ptr-write; slice(MutPtr<T>, n) remains valid.
    // Typed RawPtr aggregate/member and enum-match auto-dereferences require assert_nonnull first.
    // M5 R3: a turbofish type arg that names no declared type → positioned unknown-type (was a cc leak).
    // M5 R4: turbofish on a callee with NO type params → no-type-args (was silently dropped).
    // M5 R4: an explicit type arg that contradicts the value arg (`T=str` vs `5:i64`) → arg-type.
    // M5 R5: a closure with the WRONG param count for the FnT param → positioned arity.
    // M5 R5: a closure whose body produces the wrong type (bool where T is wanted) → arg-type.
    // Q3: calling a DEFINED non-function local is `not-callable`, distinct from `undefined-name`.
    // or_return in a fn whose return type is NOT a Result → positioned or-return error.
    // or_return whose receiver's Err type differs from the enclosing fn's Err type → or-return.
    // or_return in EXPRESSION position (return position, mid-expression) in a matching-Result fn: accepted.
    // or_return with a DIFFERING Ok payload: accepted in consume positions (let/arg/mid-chain — only E
    // must match), still or-return in conditional positions (&& rhs, @while cond), and return-fit for
    // the degenerate bare `return f().or_return()`.
    // One-armed bool matches reject with positioned exhaustiveness diagnostics in statement, value,
    // and nested-loop positions. `.then({ ... })` is the explicit one-way spelling.
    // infinite-type: a struct/enum containing ITSELF by value has infinite size (the cc-error leak fix).
    // INDIRECTION breaks the cycle — `Ptr<T>` / `[T]` are finite, so these are accepted.
    a + b
}
